{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e0649962a3599168", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Saving vs. Investing", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "A savings account is a good choice for short-term goals or to hold an emergency fund that can cover unexpected expenses.", "question": null, "answer": "Saving and investing both mean setting aside some of the money you earn, but they serve different purposes. A savings account is suited to short-term goals or emergency funds. Investing means putting money into assets such as stocks or bonds with the expectation of earning a return over time. Unlike savings, investments involve risk: their values can fluctuate, and investing does not have a set rate of return.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2887, "output_tokens": 208}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e0649962a3599168", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Saving vs. Investing", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "A savings account is a good choice for short-term goals or to hold an emergency fund that can cover unexpected expenses.", "question": "How does saving differ from investing, especially when choosing where to keep money for a short-term goal?", "answer": "Saving means setting money aside, often in a savings account suited to short-term goals or emergencies. Investing means putting money into assets such as stocks or bonds with the expectation of earning a return over time; investments involve risk and may fluctuate in value.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2887, "output_tokens": 199}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e0649962a3599168", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Saving vs. Investing", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "A savings account is a good choice for short-term goals or to hold an emergency fund that can cover unexpected expenses. ... Investing is when you put your money into assets such as stocks or bonds, often held in a brokerage or advisory account, with the expectation of making a return over time.", "question": "Misconception: Saving and investing are interchangeable because both involve setting money aside. Is that right?", "answer": "Not quite. Both involve setting aside money, but they serve different purposes: a savings account can be suitable for short-term goals or emergency funds, while investing means putting money into assets such as stocks or bonds with the expectation of a return over time. Investments also involve risk, so their value can fluctuate.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2887, "output_tokens": 250}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:5fbca6958430e1e5", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Returns", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Return from an asset may come from an increase in the asset’s value or from an asset’s interest or dividend payments to those who own it.", "question": null, "answer": "An investment return is what you gain from an asset over time. It may come from the asset increasing in value, or from interest or dividend payments. These are different ways an investment can provide a return.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 362}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:5fbca6958430e1e5", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Returns", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Return from an asset may come from an increase in the asset’s value or from an asset’s interest or dividend payments to those who own it.", "question": "What are two ways an investment can generate a return?", "answer": "An investment can generate a return if its value increases, or through interest or dividend payments to its owners.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 153}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:5fbca6958430e1e5", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Returns", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Investing doesn’t have a set rate of return", "question": "Misconception: Investing guarantees a fixed annual return, such as 7–10%.", "answer": "Investing does not have a set or guaranteed rate of return. The source describes 7–10% only as a useful estimate for long-term diversified investments in U.S. stocks based on historic averages, and notes that all investments involve risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 222}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e67814cd110fa52a", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Compound Growth", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Compound growth happens when you earn a return on money you invest as well as on the return your invested money earns.", "question": null, "answer": "Compound growth means your investment can earn returns on both the money you put in and earlier returns that remain invested. Over time, those returns can build on one another—like a snowball gathering snow as it rolls. Keeping money invested for more years can give compounding more time to work, though investment returns are not guaranteed and investments involve risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 215}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e67814cd110fa52a", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Compound Growth", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Compound growth happens when you earn a return on money you invest as well as on the return your invested money earns.", "question": "What is compound growth in investing?", "answer": "Compound growth occurs when your investment earns returns both on the original money you invested and on returns that have accumulated over time. Keeping money invested for years can allow those returns to build on one another.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 178}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e67814cd110fa52a", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Compound Growth", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Compound growth happens when you earn a return on money you invest as well as on the return your invested money earns.", "question": "Misconception: Compound growth means you earn returns only on the money you originally invested.", "answer": "Compound growth means you can earn returns both on your original investment and on returns that your invested money has already earned. Keeping money invested over time allows those returns to contribute to further growth.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 203}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4855ca6997bd5b4c", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Regular Investment Contributions", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Regular investing means that you put a set dollar amount or a set percentage of your income into your investment accounts.", "question": null, "answer": "Regular investment contributions mean putting a consistent amount—or a consistent percentage of your income—into investment accounts. Over time, keeping those funds invested can allow compound growth: you earn returns on your investment and on returns it has already earned. Starting earlier gives compounding more time to work, though the source notes that it is never too late to begin. If your income increases, increasing your regular contribution can also help build wealth over time. Investments involve risk, and returns are not guaranteed.", "formula_latex": "\\text{Regular investments} + \\text{time} \\rightarrow \\text{wealth}", "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 328}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4855ca6997bd5b4c", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Regular Investment Contributions", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Regular investing means that you put a set dollar amount or a set percentage of your income into your investment accounts.", "question": "What does it mean to make regular investment contributions?", "answer": "It means consistently putting a set dollar amount or a set percentage of your income into investment accounts. The source also explains that starting earlier gives compounding more time to work.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 186}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4855ca6997bd5b4c", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Regular Investment Contributions", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Regular investing means that you put a set dollar amount or a set percentage of your income into your investment accounts.", "question": "Misconception: Regular investing means contributing only when you have extra money left over at the end of the month.", "answer": "Regular investing means putting a set dollar amount or a set percentage of your income into investment accounts. The source also recommends planning and budgeting for contributions, including setting up automatic contributions toward long-term goals.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 202}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:48daaf04d572d5c4", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Time Horizon", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Determine your time horizon by choosing the amount of time you dedicate to reaching your goal.", "question": null, "answer": "Your investment time horizon is how long you plan to invest before using the money for a goal. For example, money invested for retirement decades away has a long time horizon. Knowing your time horizon can help you define your investing goals and consider which investment products fit your plans.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 158}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:48daaf04d572d5c4", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Time Horizon", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Determine your time horizon by choosing the amount of time you dedicate to reaching your goal.", "question": "What does an investment time horizon mean?", "answer": "Your investment time horizon is the amount of time you plan to invest before reaching a goal. Knowing it can help you develop your investing goals and choose investments alongside your risk tolerance.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 160}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:48daaf04d572d5c4", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Time Horizon", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "All investments have some degree of risk.", "question": "Misconception: If you have a long investment time horizon, market risk no longer matters.", "answer": "A long time horizon can give you time to manage market fluctuations, but it does not eliminate investment risk. Investments can still lose value, so your time horizon is one factor to consider when choosing investments.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 393}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:b3525b37c7a5f309", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "All investments have some degree of risk. Markets fluctuate over time, and individual investments may lose value.", "question": null, "answer": "Investment risk means that an investment’s value can fluctuate or decrease. You can make more informed choices by considering how long you have before you need the money and how comfortable you are with risk. Asset allocation—dividing investments among asset classes—and diversification—spreading investments across different assets—are strategies that can help manage risk. They do not eliminate the possibility of losses.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 207}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:b3525b37c7a5f309", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The idea is that if one investment loses money, other investments may make up for some of those losses.", "question": "How can diversification help manage investment risk?", "answer": "Diversification spreads investments across different kinds of assets. If one investment loses value, others may make up for some of the loss, though diversification does not eliminate all risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 158}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:b3525b37c7a5f309", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Investment Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "All investments have some degree of risk. Markets fluctuate over time, and individual investments may lose value.", "question": "Misconception: If I diversify my investments, I can’t lose money.", "answer": "Diversification can help reduce losses—for example, gains in some investments may offset losses in others—but it does not eliminate investment risk. All investments have some degree of risk, and individual investments may lose value.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 227}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4d308c5a9c02fd37", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Risk Tolerance", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Knowing your time horizon and your personal tolerance for risk is helpful. If you’re investing for a retirement that is 30 years away, you have time to manage market fluctuations. If you prefer to be more conservative with your money, that’s good to know. These factors will help you choose the types of investment products that are right for you.", "question": null, "answer": "Risk tolerance is how comfortable you are with the possibility of investment losses and market fluctuations. Along with your time horizon—the amount of time before you need the money—it can help guide which types of investment products you choose. For example, the source notes that someone who prefers a more conservative approach should take that preference into account when choosing investments.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 233}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4d308c5a9c02fd37", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Risk Tolerance", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Finding the right mix for you will involve assessing your risk tolerance and your time horizon.", "question": "How does risk tolerance help guide investment choices?", "answer": "Risk tolerance helps you assess what mix of investments may be right for you. The source explains that choosing an asset allocation involves considering both your risk tolerance and your time horizon.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 138}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:4d308c5a9c02fd37", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Risk Tolerance", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "If you prefer to be more conservative with your money, that’s good to know. These factors will help you choose the types of investment products that are right for you.", "question": "Misconception: If I prefer conservative investments, I can avoid investment risk entirely. Is that true?", "answer": "No. All investments involve some degree of risk, and investments may lose value. Knowing that you prefer a more conservative approach can help you choose investment products that fit your risk tolerance, but it does not eliminate risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2886, "output_tokens": 217}}
{"id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:f82e6e3ff28c3897", "source_id": "b4fedf3c3a0dd971", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing", "topic": "Introduction of Investing", "subtopic": "Asset Allocation", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Asset allocation involves dividing your investments among different “asset classes”, such as stocks, bonds, and cash.", "question": null, "answer": "Asset allocation means dividing your investments among asset classes, such as stocks, bonds, and cash. Choosing a mix involves considering your risk tolerance and time horizon. In other words, asset allocation is about how you spread investments across broad categories, rather than putting everything into just one type.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 2885, "output_tokens": 163}}
